```
123
```

That's because `console.log` is using under the hood [util.inspect](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html#util_util_inspect_object_options) which apparently will call `inspect` method on given object if finds one. 

This behaviour is very suprising and looks like a bug to someone who didn't read that particular doc. As a matter of fact I also [maintain library](https://github.com/szwacz/fs-jetpack) which has `inspect` method as part of its API. So doing console.log(myLib) will lead to obscure error.

**Solution?**
Both APIs [console](https://nodejs.org/api/console.html) and [util](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html) have status stable so I believe there is no way to alter this behaviour.
But how about starting favouring `toString` over `inspect`?
So this code...

```
console.log({
    inspect: function () {
        return 123;
    },
    toString: function () {
        return 'foo';
    },
});
```

...will print `foo` instead of `123`.

Then at least I'll be able to define `toString` method and avoid nasty error for the users of my library.

